{
 "id": "1960-lynch-city",
 "name": "City image sketch map and interview (Lynch 1960, The Image of the City)",
 "description": "Faithful replication, as a self-administered page, of the office interview behind Kevin Lynch's The Image of the City (MIT Press, 1960). Participants name the city they live or work in, say what first comes to mind and which physical characteristics they associate with it, draw a quick sketch map from memory (about 15 minutes; freehand drawing with name labels, the drawing order is stored because Lynch recorded the sequence in which elements were drawn), mark north, describe their usual trip step by step with what they see, hear and smell, name the elements they find most distinctive and describe up to three of them (\"taken there blindfolded…\"), and say whether the city is easy to find one's way in. Analysis as in Lynch: classify what is drawn and mentioned into paths, edges, districts, nodes and landmarks and count how many respondents include each element to build the public image. Question wording follows the project's own interview guide, \"Explanation of City-Form Interview Procedure\" (Perceptual Form of the City project, MIT, 1956; MIT Libraries Dome, handle 1721.3/35618); the book's Appendix B was not consulted, so wording may differ slightly from the printed version. The template is taken down if the rights holders object. Differences from the original study: (1) typed answers instead of a tape-recorded interview with follow-up probes; (2) the map is drawn on screen, not on tracing paper; (3) the guide's sections on general words for city areas (2), the second, interviewer-chosen trip (6) and most of the closing discussion (9) are left out; (4) the participant chooses the city (a researcher can fix it in the page); gender and age are added; Lynch's field walks and photo recognition are not included.",
